174:We develop efficient parameterized, with additive error, approximation algorithms for the (Connected) $r$-Domination problem and the (Connected) $p$-Center problem for unweighted and undirected graphs.
175:Given a graph~$G$, we show how to construct a (connected) $\big(r + \calO(\mu) \big)$-dominating set~$D$ with $|D| \leq |D^*|$ efficiently.
176:Here, $D^*$ is a minimum (connected) $r$-dominating set of~$G$ and $\mu$ is our graph parameter, which is the \emph{tree-breadth} or the \emph{cluster diameter in a layering partition} of~$G$.
177:Additionally, we show that a $+ \calO(\mu)$-approximation for the (Connected) $p$-Center problem on~$G$ can be computed in polynomial time.
178:Our interest in these parameters stems from the fact that in many real-world networks, including Internet application networks, web networks, collaboration networks, social networks, biological networks, and others, and in many structured classes of graphs these parameters are small constants.
181:\section{Introduction}
185:The \emph{Connected \( r \)-Domination problem} asks to find an $r$-dominating set~$D$ of minimum cardinality with an additional requirement that $D$ needs to induce a connected subgraph of~$G$.
189:It is easy to see also that finding minimum connected dominating sets is equivalent to finding spanning trees with the maximum possible number of leaves.
194:The domination problem is one of the most well-studied NP-hard problems in algorithmic graph theory.
195:To cope with the intractability of this problem it has been studied both in terms of approximability (relaxing the optimality) and fixed-parameter tractability (relaxing the runtime).
196:From the approximability prospective, a logarithmic approximation factor can be found by using a simple greedy algorithm, and finding a sublogarithmic approximation factor is NP-hard~\cite{RaSa1997}.
198:The Domination problem is notorious also in the theory of fixed-parameter tractability (see, \eg, \cite{DoFe1999,Niederm2006} for an introduction to parameterized complexity).
202:The $p$-Center problem is known to be NP-hard on graphs.
203:However, for it, a simple and efficient factor~$2$ approximation algorithm exists~\cite{Gon1985}.
204:Furthermore, it is a best possible approximation algorithm in the sense that an approximation with factor less than~$2$ is proven to be NP-hard (see~\cite{Gon1985} for more details).
205:The NP-hardness of the Connected $p$-Center problem is shown in~\cite{YenChen2007}.
207:Recently, in~\cite{ChepoiEstell2007}, a new type of approximability result (call it a \emph{parameterized approximability} result) was obtained:
208:there exists a polynomial time algorithm which finds in an arbitrary graph~$G$ having a minimum $r$-dominating set~$D$ a set~$D'$ such that $|D'| \leq |D|$ and each vertex~$v \in V$ is within distance at most~$r(v) + 2 \delta$ from~$D'$, where $\delta$ is the hyperbolicity parameter of~$G$ (see~\cite{ChepoiEstell2007} for details).
209:We call such a~$D'$ an \emph{\( (r + 2 \delta) \)-dominating set} of~$G$.
212:We call such a~$C'$ a \emph{\( + 6\delta \)-approximation for the \( p \)-Center problem}.
215:Unfortunately, the results of~\cite{ChepoiEstell2007,EdwaKennSani2016} are hardly extendable to connected versions of the $r$-Domination and $p$-Center problems.
216:It remains an open question whether similar approximability results parameterized by the graph's hyperbolicity can be obtained for the Connected $r$-Domination and Connected $p$-Center problems.
217:Instead, we consider two other graph parameters:
219:Both parameters (like the hyperbolicity) capture the metric tree-likeness of a graph (see, \eg, \cite{AbuAtaDragan2016} and papers cited therein).
220:As demonstrated in~\cite{AbuAtaDragan2016}, in many real-world networks, including Internet application networks, web networks, collaboration networks, social networks, biological networks, and others, as well as in many structured classes of graphs the parameters $\delta$, $\rho$, and~$\Delta$ are small constants.
222:We show here that, for a given $n$-vertex, $m$-edge graph~$G$, having a minimum $r$-dominating set~$D$ and a minimum connected $r$-dominating set~$C$:
225:        an $(r + \Delta)$-dominating set~$D'$ with $|D'| \leq |D|$ can be computed in linear time;
227:        a connected $(r + 2 \Delta)$-dominating set~$C'$ with $|C'| \leq |C|$ can be computed in $\calO \big( m \, \alpha(n) \log \Delta \big)$ time (where $\alpha(n)$ is the inverse Ackermann function);
229:        a $+ \Delta$-approximation for the $p$-Center problem can be computed in linear time;
231:        a $+ 2 \Delta$-approximation for the connected $p$-Center problem can be computed in $\calO \big( m \, \alpha(n) \log \min(\Delta, p) \big)$ time.
237:        an $(r + \rho)$-dominating set~$D'$ with $|D'| \leq |D|$ can be computed in $\calO(nm)$ time;
239:        a connected $\big( r + 5 \rho \big)$-dominating set~$C'$ with $|C'| \leq |C|$ can be computed in $\calO(nm)$ time;
241:        a $+ \rho$-approximation for the $p$-Center problem can be computed in $\calO( nm \log n)$ time;
243:        a $+ 5 \rho$-approximation for the Connected $p$-Center problem can be computed in $\calO( nm \log n)$ time.
248:Note that, for split graphs (graphs in which the vertices can be partitioned into a clique and an independent set), all three parameters are at most~$1$.
249:Additionally, as shown in~\cite{ChlebiChlebi2008}, there is (under reasonable assumptions) no polynomial-time algorithm to compute a sublogarithmic-factor approximation for the (Connected) Domination problem in split graphs.
252:One can extend this result to show that there is no polynomial-time algorithm~$\calA$ which computes, for any constant~$c$, a $+ c \log n$-approximation for split graphs.
253:Hence, there is no polynomial-time $+ c \Delta \log n$-approximation algorithm in general.
259:If there is such an algorithm~$\calA$, then $\calA$ produces a (connected) dominating set~$D_\calA$ for~$H$ which hast at most $2 c \log n$ more vertices that a minimum (connected) dominating set~$D$.
261:Therefore, such an algorithm~$\calA$ would allow to solve the (Connected) Domination problem for split graphs in polynomial time.
263:\section{Preliminaries}
275:A vertex set~$D$ is called an \emph{\( r \)-dominating set} of~$G$ if each vertex~$u \in V$ is $r$ dominated by~$D$.
277:An \emph{\( (r + \phi) \)-dominating set} is defined accordingly.
278:For a given graph~$G$ and function~$r$, the \emph{(Connected) \( r \)-Domination} problem asks for the smallest (connected) vertex set~$D$ such that $D$ is an $r$-dominating set of~$G$.
